<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muawiya I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/661">661</a>–<a href="/years/680">680 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, Muawiya, Muawiyah, Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muawiya I was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, ruling from 661 to 680. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali in the first Muslim civil war, and after Ali's death he became caliph, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>. He moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and established hereditary succession by nominating his son Yazid.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muawiya-i.jpg" alt="Muawiya I" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LouisAragon (uploader) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab-Sasanian_coin_of_Muawiyah_I,_struck_at_the_Fasa_mint_in_Darabjird_(Fars).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Early Islamic sources give Mu'awiya's year of birth as 597, 603 or 605. His father, Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, was a prominent Meccan merchant who led trade caravans to Byzantine Syria and became leader of the Banu Abd Shams clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>. Mu'awiya and his father opposed Muhammad until Muhammad captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 630, after which Mu'awiya became one of his scribes. Caliph Abu Bakr appointed him a deputy commander in the conquest of Syria, and he rose through the caliphate of Umar until Uthman made him governor of Syria. There he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Banu Kalb</a> tribe, marrying a daughter of its ruling house about 650. He also launched the first Muslim naval campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, with Uthman's permission, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Uthman was killed in 656, Mu'awiya took up the cause of avenging him and withheld allegiance from Ali. Their armies met at Siffin in 657 and reached a stalemate, and arbitration talks failed. In April or May 658 the Syrians pledged allegiance to Mu'awiya as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, and his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from Ali's governor.</p><p>After Ali was killed in 661, Mu'awiya marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, where Hasan, Ali's son, abdicated in return for a financial settlement. Mu'awiya entered the city in July or September 661, and early sources call that year the year of unity.</p><p>He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> his capital and moved the treasury there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>. He relied on a Syrian tribal army of about 100,000 men and ruled the provinces indirectly through governors with full civil and military authority, preferring agreements and bribes to confrontation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> became a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>, and almost every year his troops raided Byzantine territory across the Anatolian frontier. In 670 the commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> began the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>. He nominated his son Yazid as his successor, a move without precedent, making the caliphate hereditary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mu'awiya died of an illness in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in April or May 680, at about the age of 80, and was buried by the Bab al-Saghir gate. The surviving Muslim histories come from Abbasid-era Iraq and are mostly hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, yet they portray him in a relatively balanced way, stressing his hilm, meaning mildness and the skilful management of people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> do not count him among the rightly-guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, since he gained the caliphate through civil war and made it hereditary, but they honour him as a companion of Muhammad and a scribe of the revelation. In Shia Islam he is reviled for opposing Ali.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mu'awiya I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ali ibn Abi Talib’s troubled caliphate from 656 to 661 CE was dominated by the First Fitna, the civil war between Ali and the governor of Syria, <b>Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan</b>, over responsibility for Uthman’s murder.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan</b>, the long-serving governor of Syria and the most powerful figure in the Islamic world, then secured recognition as caliph.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At Siffin on the Euphrates, Caliph Ali ibn Abi Talib fought <b>Muawiya</b>, governor of Syria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As Arab armies overran the Sasanian lands, the Armenian commander Theodore Rshtuni made a treaty with <b>Muawiya</b> in 653 accepting Arab overlordship in return for autonomy and keeping Armenian troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-armenia">Principality of Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=670" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muawiya I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=670">What was here in 670 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
